WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.860 --> 00:00:06.915
&gt;&gt; In this session,
we'll have two talks.

00:00:06.916 --> 00:00:08.850
One will be a continuation of

00:00:08.851 --> 00:00:11.630
what we discussed in
the last workshop,

00:00:11.631 --> 00:00:14.340
and then folks from

00:00:14.341 --> 00:00:15.870
Sunita will give
an introduction to what

00:00:15.871 --> 00:00:18.330
happens in the next session

00:00:18.331 --> 00:00:21.265
where we have NGO's
would present problems.

00:00:21.266 --> 00:00:22.590
Okay, I'm very pleased to

00:00:22.591 --> 00:00:25.465
introduce Professor
Ganesh Ramakrishnan.

00:00:25.466 --> 00:00:27.750
This is a project

00:00:27.751 --> 00:00:30.180
that got many of us inspired
at the last workshop,

00:00:30.181 --> 00:00:33.390
which we held at
ISC, and the goal is

00:00:33.391 --> 00:00:34.440
to think about problem

00:00:34.441 --> 00:00:36.680
solving in a civic
complaint sense.

00:00:36.681 --> 00:00:38.180
All right. So you all have had

00:00:38.181 --> 00:00:40.070
problems where you see
portraits on the road,

00:00:40.071 --> 00:00:41.285
you see things that don't work

00:00:41.286 --> 00:00:43.115
in your city where you live,

00:00:43.116 --> 00:00:45.095
and it's often very hard to know

00:00:45.096 --> 00:00:47.420
who to contact, how to complain,

00:00:47.421 --> 00:00:49.790
and once you make a complaint,
how to follow back

00:00:49.791 --> 00:00:52.320
and understand whether
it got solved or not.

00:00:52.321 --> 00:00:55.190
Right. And so the Venter solution

00:00:55.191 --> 00:00:56.540
that they proposed last year

00:00:56.541 --> 00:00:58.700
was a technology solution to

00:00:58.701 --> 00:01:01.135
help you make complaints easily,

00:01:01.136 --> 00:01:04.160
and then also help you
understand and look

00:01:04.161 --> 00:01:07.315
at all the complaints for
a city manager or for us.

00:01:07.316 --> 00:01:10.980
I'm very excited to hear
updates from Dr. Ganesh.

00:01:10.981 --> 00:01:14.125
&gt;&gt; Thanks for hosting
this workshop,

00:01:14.126 --> 00:01:17.465
and I'm glad to see
several of you back here.

00:01:17.466 --> 00:01:19.900
So, this is joint work with

00:01:19.901 --> 00:01:22.060
Sunita Sarawagi faculty
at IIT Bombay,

00:01:22.061 --> 00:01:26.815
and then a couple of students,
graduates, project staff.

00:01:26.816 --> 00:01:32.290
This is AI-based complaint
management motivation.

00:01:32.291 --> 00:01:35.645
There are several venting
platforms that are extremely

00:01:35.646 --> 00:01:39.640
operational flooded with
complains, IChangeMyCity,

00:01:39.641 --> 00:01:44.440
SpeakUp, Mumbai basically has

00:01:44.441 --> 00:01:48.155
a Complaint Management
System for its own.

00:01:48.156 --> 00:01:50.540
Inspired by all of that,
IIT Bombay has also

00:01:50.541 --> 00:01:52.355
started a complaint
management system,

00:01:52.356 --> 00:01:55.380
and there are many others.

00:01:55.381 --> 00:01:57.455
I've highlighted
the first three who

00:01:57.456 --> 00:02:00.550
actually are active clients.

00:02:00.551 --> 00:02:04.115
One of the complains that
are images, there is text.

00:02:04.116 --> 00:02:06.170
Text can be short, text could

00:02:06.171 --> 00:02:09.125
have multiple languages
and also mixed code.

00:02:09.126 --> 00:02:13.360
Locations, though we have
a separate field for location,

00:02:13.361 --> 00:02:14.450
many of these services do

00:02:14.451 --> 00:02:16.345
provide separate fields
for locations,

00:02:16.346 --> 00:02:18.140
people tend to get all of

00:02:18.141 --> 00:02:20.525
that mixed up in
the actual description.

00:02:20.526 --> 00:02:22.935
You can see the
descriptions are all,

00:02:22.936 --> 00:02:24.290
with images are they're not

00:02:24.291 --> 00:02:26.870
completely consistent
with the image itself.

00:02:26.871 --> 00:02:28.130
So, these are basically

00:02:28.131 --> 00:02:29.770
concerns about cows
eating garbage,

00:02:29.771 --> 00:02:33.155
but the text is
something different.

00:02:33.156 --> 00:02:35.245
And these are all from
Bangalore incidentally.

00:02:35.246 --> 00:02:43.170
So, what's the scale
of these services?

00:02:43.171 --> 00:02:44.715
IChangeMyCity with

00:02:44.716 --> 00:02:47.400
eight million users
across several cities,

00:02:47.401 --> 00:02:49.520
and the range of problems,

00:02:49.521 --> 00:02:52.890
lots of different across
these different organizations.

00:02:52.891 --> 00:02:57.330
There are also issue such
as fake or spam posts,

00:02:57.331 --> 00:02:58.715
which are very specific

00:02:58.716 --> 00:03:00.975
to certain organizations
and services.

00:03:00.976 --> 00:03:03.075
MCGM, on the other hand,

00:03:03.076 --> 00:03:05.180
doesn't have
a very nice interface.

00:03:05.181 --> 00:03:08.630
It does provide an interface
for typing in text,

00:03:08.631 --> 00:03:12.450
but it has an excellent
complaint routing system.

00:03:12.451 --> 00:03:16.070
Each offers a strings
and has limitations.

00:03:16.071 --> 00:03:18.785
SpeakUp, again these
are of Bangalore,

00:03:18.786 --> 00:03:22.340
is India's first one-stop
feedback platform

00:03:22.341 --> 00:03:24.875
not necessarily restricted
to civic problems,

00:03:24.876 --> 00:03:27.660
but also banking related
problems, Aadhaar problems.

00:03:27.661 --> 00:03:30.785
So they have
several verticals and

00:03:30.786 --> 00:03:32.060
feedbacks and all analyze

00:03:32.061 --> 00:03:34.900
manually as in
most other services.

00:03:34.901 --> 00:03:37.460
Sometimes a feedback tend
to be extremely

00:03:37.461 --> 00:03:42.840
emotional so sentiment analysis
becomes quite important,

00:03:42.841 --> 00:03:46.100
and there are several
junk oppose as well.

00:03:46.101 --> 00:03:48.180
Just to give you
an idea, this is a kind

00:03:48.181 --> 00:03:50.245
of contrast SpeakUp complaints.

00:03:50.246 --> 00:03:52.640
This is about
high-speed Internet.

00:03:52.641 --> 00:03:55.280
This, I think about
some telephone provider.

00:03:55.281 --> 00:03:56.480
On the other hand, ICMC is

00:03:56.481 --> 00:03:58.340
largely about civic complaints.

00:03:58.341 --> 00:04:00.170
So obviously, we can't

00:04:00.171 --> 00:04:02.550
provide a solution that
works across all of them.

00:04:02.551 --> 00:04:04.790
What we've been evolving is

00:04:04.791 --> 00:04:06.470
a platform which all of us could

00:04:06.471 --> 00:04:08.975
use and in fact
tune their models.

00:04:08.976 --> 00:04:12.930
So, here's how things
were some time back.

00:04:12.931 --> 00:04:16.520
The users, there are
these venting platforms

00:04:16.521 --> 00:04:19.015
and the civic bodies to whom

00:04:19.016 --> 00:04:21.760
these platforms are
routed interestingly.

00:04:21.761 --> 00:04:23.900
ICMC routes when several of

00:04:23.901 --> 00:04:27.320
its complaints related
to Mumbai to MCGM.

00:04:27.370 --> 00:04:30.355
So, where does venter come in?

00:04:30.356 --> 00:04:34.860
So, venter also could
stand for vent analyzer.

00:04:34.861 --> 00:04:36.740
There are existing
venting platforms

00:04:36.741 --> 00:04:40.915
and what we do is use AI

00:04:40.916 --> 00:04:44.090
so that citizens
could connect to each

00:04:44.091 --> 00:04:47.225
other through a common
shared label space,

00:04:47.226 --> 00:04:50.000
so that we realize
two complaints are exactly about

00:04:50.001 --> 00:04:53.965
the same issue possibly in
the same location as well,

00:04:53.966 --> 00:04:56.070
and also providing tools

00:04:56.071 --> 00:04:57.710
for venting platform
to control the AI.

00:04:57.711 --> 00:04:59.570
The basically two issues A and B,

00:04:59.571 --> 00:05:01.475
I'm going to use those numbers.

00:05:01.476 --> 00:05:03.050
So, A is connecting

00:05:03.051 --> 00:05:05.060
citizens to others with
the same complaint,

00:05:05.061 --> 00:05:07.745
and B are actually tools that we

00:05:07.746 --> 00:05:11.515
have been providing
these clients,

00:05:11.516 --> 00:05:15.400
ICMC, SpeakUp, and so on.

00:05:15.401 --> 00:05:17.705
So that they could
build your own models.

00:05:17.706 --> 00:05:21.155
How interactive label
and increment labeling?

00:05:21.156 --> 00:05:26.120
Explainable labeling, and
what's all the capability

00:05:26.121 --> 00:05:28.325
of designing
simple rules which we

00:05:28.326 --> 00:05:30.895
more generally call
labeling functions,

00:05:30.896 --> 00:05:34.400
and the whole problem
of label sets evolving

00:05:34.401 --> 00:05:36.080
because nothing is static and

00:05:36.081 --> 00:05:38.915
fossilized for any of
these organizations.

00:05:38.916 --> 00:05:41.550
So, the role that
MSR has been playing

00:05:41.551 --> 00:05:44.470
is helping us identify and
connect to social partners.

00:05:44.471 --> 00:05:46.180
It has been really handy. Who
would have known who knows

00:05:46.181 --> 00:05:48.290
SpeakUp and the issues they had?

00:05:48.291 --> 00:05:50.885
Also helping create the bridge

00:05:50.886 --> 00:05:53.170
for a dialogue is
also challenging.

00:05:53.171 --> 00:05:56.210
This has taken several rounds
of iterations for us to get

00:05:56.211 --> 00:06:01.140
some steady-state involvement
with IChangedMyCity.

00:06:01.880 --> 00:06:05.330
Recently, we also
came to know that

00:06:05.331 --> 00:06:08.510
Amazon has a CC text feedback
classification system.

00:06:08.511 --> 00:06:10.535
We started to some extent,

00:06:10.536 --> 00:06:14.230
it's what a more
restricted vertical,

00:06:14.231 --> 00:06:16.790
but we're really happy to
collaborate the entire week.

00:06:16.791 --> 00:06:19.090
It will be very happy
for MSR Sys Sieve

00:06:19.091 --> 00:06:21.260
to own up all that you've done.

00:06:21.540 --> 00:06:24.710
So, our contribution so far

00:06:24.711 --> 00:06:25.830
has been building AI tools and

00:06:25.831 --> 00:06:27.460
platforms which are being used.

00:06:27.461 --> 00:06:29.790
We also want to connect to

00:06:29.791 --> 00:06:31.500
the industrial
experience and MSR to

00:06:31.501 --> 00:06:33.175
actually scale it up.

00:06:33.176 --> 00:06:35.010
We've been scaling
out to some extent,

00:06:35.011 --> 00:06:37.080
but scaling up is a challenge.

00:06:37.081 --> 00:06:39.510
So, one of the AI challenges

00:06:39.511 --> 00:06:41.110
here, complaints are multi-modal,

00:06:41.111 --> 00:06:42.280
this text plus image,

00:06:42.281 --> 00:06:44.250
and as I said there may be
inconsistency between what's

00:06:44.251 --> 00:06:46.835
tight and what's there in image,

00:06:46.836 --> 00:06:49.260
noisy, mixed-language complaints,

00:06:49.261 --> 00:06:50.795
"Koi garbage nahi utatha",

00:06:50.796 --> 00:06:54.850
and evolving set of issues,

00:06:54.920 --> 00:06:59.040
just to give you an idea when
we use some deep networks,

00:06:59.041 --> 00:07:01.505
anything that is pulled out

00:07:01.506 --> 00:07:05.040
off the shelf doesn't really
work in the Indian context.

00:07:05.041 --> 00:07:07.760
So for example, for dogs,
stray, roaming dogs,

00:07:07.761 --> 00:07:10.865
etc, are what we learn.

00:07:10.866 --> 00:07:13.000
Whereas if you use
pre-trained embeddings,

00:07:13.001 --> 00:07:15.410
you have zebra related to a dog,

00:07:15.411 --> 00:07:19.940
but as far as zebra would be
more related to a crossing.

00:07:19.941 --> 00:07:27.970
Likewise, for garbage, and
these are more examples,

00:07:27.971 --> 00:07:29.670
and there are a lot of

00:07:29.671 --> 00:07:31.380
sub-tasks that are shared
across complaints.

00:07:31.381 --> 00:07:33.905
For example, this is
where we generalize

00:07:33.906 --> 00:07:37.400
our solutions across
the different users and clients.

00:07:37.401 --> 00:07:41.540
The methodology that can
help them extract problems

00:07:41.541 --> 00:07:43.500
and separate them out
from entities about

00:07:43.501 --> 00:07:46.385
waste or problem is stated.

00:07:46.386 --> 00:07:49.575
We embed all of these
in a continuous space.

00:07:49.576 --> 00:07:52.040
Interestingly, we're also able to

00:07:52.041 --> 00:07:54.740
embed images in the same
continuous space as a text.

00:07:54.741 --> 00:07:58.510
I have sometimes complaints

00:07:58.511 --> 00:08:01.580
of only images, that's
another challenge.

00:08:02.670 --> 00:08:05.950
That's where
this co-embedding helps.

00:08:05.951 --> 00:08:06.390
So, here are examples.

00:08:06.391 --> 00:08:08.725
The examples speak
louder than words.

00:08:08.726 --> 00:08:11.135
So, we were able
to figure out that

00:08:11.136 --> 00:08:15.170
these images are
all about garbage,

00:08:15.171 --> 00:08:16.940
but they are in a specific sense

00:08:16.941 --> 00:08:18.290
of garbage that they're all

00:08:18.291 --> 00:08:21.560
related to cows eating
up this plastic trash,

00:08:21.561 --> 00:08:23.210
which is concerns that

00:08:23.211 --> 00:08:28.120
responsible citizens have
shared on the platform.

00:08:29.200 --> 00:08:33.360
This is another interesting case
"kunda pada hai".

00:08:33.500 --> 00:08:36.590
Now, you can see that

00:08:36.591 --> 00:08:41.780
the text actually varies
significantly across depots.

00:08:41.781 --> 00:08:43.370
This has been here for months.

00:08:43.371 --> 00:08:45.515
People have started
dumping more garbage,

00:08:45.516 --> 00:08:47.970
whereas near garg stores,

00:08:47.971 --> 00:08:48.980
sarai pipal thala, which has

00:08:48.981 --> 00:08:51.670
no mention of
the word garbage at all.

00:08:51.671 --> 00:08:55.275
So, all these have been mapped
to the garbage category.

00:08:55.276 --> 00:08:57.710
Okay. So, what do
we have currently?

00:08:57.711 --> 00:09:00.085
Venter service which is exposed,

00:09:00.086 --> 00:09:03.340
which is acceptable to decide,

00:09:04.170 --> 00:09:07.340
any of these venting
platforms comes to us.

00:09:07.341 --> 00:09:08.580
This is great on a conference,

00:09:08.581 --> 00:09:10.220
we can do that right away,

00:09:10.221 --> 00:09:12.555
and specifically, within IIT,

00:09:12.556 --> 00:09:16.039
we're also experimenting
with app-based interfaces

00:09:16.040 --> 00:09:17.610
because most of the other

00:09:17.611 --> 00:09:19.875
venting platforms
have their own apps.

00:09:19.876 --> 00:09:22.880
But what we realize

00:09:22.881 --> 00:09:25.325
is these apps also need
to be empowered with AI.

00:09:25.326 --> 00:09:28.010
These tags should be
interactively suggested.

00:09:28.011 --> 00:09:29.150
Can the user correct

00:09:29.151 --> 00:09:30.750
the grammar of
the complaint as he types?

00:09:30.751 --> 00:09:33.590
No, all that, we really can't
experiment with some of

00:09:33.591 --> 00:09:36.770
these operational
venting platform.

00:09:36.771 --> 00:09:39.125
So, we decided to do
that in-house as well.

00:09:39.126 --> 00:09:40.130
I'm going to run you through

00:09:40.131 --> 00:09:41.390
a very quick video
because that'll give

00:09:41.391 --> 00:09:44.110
you everything that is
required to understand.

00:09:44.111 --> 00:09:47.470
This what happens, any
of these organizations

00:09:47.471 --> 00:09:52.210
can login with
the account as provided.

00:09:53.360 --> 00:09:56.400
We use the "Category
Prediction Model".

00:09:56.401 --> 00:09:58.930
We kept all the
interaction through

00:09:58.931 --> 00:10:00.220
simple CSV files because

00:10:00.221 --> 00:10:02.700
that's where they find
it most effective.

00:10:03.010 --> 00:10:06.050
We've gone through iterations
to get to you here.

00:10:06.051 --> 00:10:09.990
So, in the past, we will
ask them to use our APIs,

00:10:09.991 --> 00:10:11.740
but that didn't work out given

00:10:11.741 --> 00:10:14.455
the very nature of
these implementations.

00:10:14.456 --> 00:10:17.420
Here, what you see is the

00:10:17.421 --> 00:10:19.210
spreadsheet that
they have uploaded.

00:10:19.211 --> 00:10:20.660
You can see the complaints
and there are

00:10:20.661 --> 00:10:23.730
spheres are very specific
to that organization.

00:10:25.360 --> 00:10:27.830
They have their
complaint types based

00:10:27.831 --> 00:10:29.500
on which we learn
a complaint classifier,

00:10:29.501 --> 00:10:35.390
and now what our system

00:10:35.391 --> 00:10:38.910
does is once it trains
the model for any new complain,

00:10:38.911 --> 00:10:40.830
it will give you a prediction,

00:10:40.831 --> 00:10:47.930
the complaint type with
the confidence of that class,

00:10:47.931 --> 00:10:48.470
so you can see there are

00:10:48.471 --> 00:10:50.040
multiple classes
at drop down list.

00:10:50.041 --> 00:10:55.145
The venting platform,

00:10:55.146 --> 00:10:57.910
the admin there would
actually correct the label,

00:10:57.911 --> 00:11:00.450
and this corrected label
will reflect

00:11:00.451 --> 00:11:05.545
in the model that gets updated
after a round of training.

00:11:05.546 --> 00:11:08.660
Very something very simplistic
that they're able to use,

00:11:08.661 --> 00:11:10.310
all this as I said
is making use of

00:11:10.311 --> 00:11:13.790
the learning images and
texts both are handled,

00:11:13.791 --> 00:11:15.650
though we have shown
only the text part in

00:11:15.651 --> 00:11:19.140
this demo for the sake
of simplicity.

00:11:19.780 --> 00:11:22.190
So, you can see, and there

00:11:22.191 --> 00:11:24.275
is also color coding
that is used to

00:11:24.276 --> 00:11:31.390
improve the user
experience. Okay.

00:11:31.391 --> 00:11:33.530
The user can actually upload,

00:11:33.531 --> 00:11:35.620
and then the whole thing
gets reflected in

00:11:35.621 --> 00:11:38.180
the next round of classification.

00:11:38.181 --> 00:11:40.295
So, we have likewise in InstiApp.

00:11:40.296 --> 00:11:42.890
Now, the interesting thing
about InstiApp is,

00:11:42.891 --> 00:11:44.590
even as the user
types the complaint,

00:11:44.591 --> 00:11:47.205
he's able to see
the possible labels,

00:11:47.206 --> 00:11:50.440
and the user can actually select,

00:11:50.441 --> 00:11:51.910
or unselect one of

00:11:51.911 --> 00:11:53.290
the predicted labels based on

00:11:53.291 --> 00:11:54.940
what he thinks is appropriate.

00:11:54.941 --> 00:11:57.040
So, there's a lot of

00:11:57.041 --> 00:12:00.140
semantics lying embodied
in a label, right?

00:12:00.141 --> 00:12:01.720
What do you mean by garbage?

00:12:01.721 --> 00:12:03.000
It's very different across

00:12:03.001 --> 00:12:05.130
people, you're quite surprised.

00:12:05.131 --> 00:12:08.210
So, an electric pole
that has fallen

00:12:08.211 --> 00:12:09.680
down because of rains

00:12:09.681 --> 00:12:11.435
becomes garbage for
some people, right?

00:12:11.436 --> 00:12:15.965
So, consistency is
the key issue here.

00:12:15.966 --> 00:12:19.220
So, this is how
the InstiApp looks like.

00:12:19.221 --> 00:12:21.590
As I said, along with the text,

00:12:21.591 --> 00:12:22.940
there is also tags that come up

00:12:22.941 --> 00:12:24.530
so that we're able
to come up with

00:12:24.531 --> 00:12:26.620
a consensus of
the tax as quickly as

00:12:26.621 --> 00:12:30.000
possible without
a long turnaround time.

00:12:30.001 --> 00:12:32.680
The interface that we showed,

00:12:32.681 --> 00:12:34.360
there's a turnaround
time that gets in

00:12:34.361 --> 00:12:36.110
code because the services

00:12:36.111 --> 00:12:39.790
become disconnected from
the actual platform.

00:12:39.791 --> 00:12:42.320
So, you're trying to do
the tight integration here.

00:12:42.321 --> 00:12:45.340
Okay. So, what are

00:12:45.341 --> 00:12:46.640
the issues? So, I said
there are two things.

00:12:46.641 --> 00:12:47.690
What we have already deployed,

00:12:47.691 --> 00:12:49.060
and these are problems
we're working

00:12:49.061 --> 00:12:51.210
on for individual organizations.

00:12:51.211 --> 00:12:52.780
One of the issues is

00:12:52.781 --> 00:12:56.045
involving complaint
classification and ontology.

00:12:56.046 --> 00:12:58.955
The label site keeps changing.

00:12:58.956 --> 00:13:02.020
So, how can we use AI to let

00:13:02.021 --> 00:13:03.250
the venting platforms evolve

00:13:03.251 --> 00:13:04.360
their own classifiers and

00:13:04.361 --> 00:13:06.150
evolve their own
class labels as well?

00:13:06.151 --> 00:13:08.410
So, here you can see entities

00:13:08.411 --> 00:13:10.050
like roads, garbage,
animal, streetlights,

00:13:10.051 --> 00:13:13.220
and suddenly, drones become
new set of entities,

00:13:13.221 --> 00:13:14.880
and that's a reality.

00:13:14.881 --> 00:13:16.465
At least in the larger cities,

00:13:16.466 --> 00:13:19.270
and then rallies, again
in larger cities.

00:13:19.271 --> 00:13:21.340
So, you need to associate

00:13:21.341 --> 00:13:24.330
new complain
categories like noisy,

00:13:24.331 --> 00:13:26.245
and beggars asking
money, and so on.

00:13:26.246 --> 00:13:28.710
In fact, beggars asking money is

00:13:28.711 --> 00:13:29.860
a new problem associated with

00:13:29.861 --> 00:13:32.900
an old entity that's garbage.

00:13:33.000 --> 00:13:36.235
So, you're obviously going
to have the same label set,

00:13:36.236 --> 00:13:37.610
catered for all time,

00:13:37.611 --> 00:13:39.910
for all organizations.
So, how do they evolve?

00:13:39.911 --> 00:13:42.090
So, we have some solutions here,

00:13:42.091 --> 00:13:44.510
and also recency matters.

00:13:44.511 --> 00:13:47.400
So, what I meant by garbage
at the beginning of creation,

00:13:47.401 --> 00:13:49.295
or rather when I
started my platform,

00:13:49.296 --> 00:13:51.980
is probably different from
what I mean right now.

00:13:51.981 --> 00:13:54.780
So, how do we give
recency more importance?

00:13:54.781 --> 00:13:58.810
Again, we use
labeled memory networks

00:13:58.811 --> 00:14:01.090
which incorporate a mixture of

00:14:01.091 --> 00:14:03.425
simple nearest neighbor
classification,

00:14:03.426 --> 00:14:07.985
and the long-term learning
across the legacy data set.

00:14:07.986 --> 00:14:09.980
The other thing is finding

00:14:09.981 --> 00:14:11.630
an extraction of problems
about an entity.

00:14:11.631 --> 00:14:13.375
So, what.

00:14:13.376 --> 00:14:18.175
&gt;&gt; So, in different platforms,
and they give you data,

00:14:18.176 --> 00:14:19.510
do you continue to update

00:14:19.511 --> 00:14:21.670
your own models
collecting from all of

00:14:21.671 --> 00:14:23.350
them like what are

00:14:23.351 --> 00:14:25.150
the data rights and how
do they react to that?

00:14:25.151 --> 00:14:28.505
&gt;&gt; So, we provide a checkbox.

00:14:28.506 --> 00:14:31.690
We ask the organization
to let us know

00:14:31.691 --> 00:14:34.775
if we could use the data
they're going to in use, right?

00:14:34.776 --> 00:14:36.730
So, we need to work
with you on how to

00:14:36.731 --> 00:14:38.860
create stricter access controls,

00:14:38.861 --> 00:14:40.010
but for the time
being, they're all

00:14:40.011 --> 00:14:41.750
happy with this arrangement,
they trust us.

00:14:41.751 --> 00:14:43.860
So, only if they
click that checkbox,

00:14:43.861 --> 00:14:47.500
we use it to update our model,
otherwise it's theirs.

00:14:47.501 --> 00:14:48.520
Thanks. I would really like to

00:14:48.521 --> 00:14:51.440
know the right ways
to go about it.

00:14:52.860 --> 00:14:55.390
The other thing is why he wants

00:14:55.391 --> 00:14:58.720
a particular label assigned
to a particular complaint.

00:14:58.721 --> 00:15:01.120
That's all about finding
entity extraction,

00:15:01.121 --> 00:15:02.650
highlighting sections
in the complaint

00:15:02.651 --> 00:15:04.625
that led to a particular label.

00:15:04.626 --> 00:15:07.955
So, garbage not
collected versus garbage

00:15:07.956 --> 00:15:11.895
blocking vehicle regular
traffic on a road,

00:15:11.896 --> 00:15:13.585
a very different complaint.

00:15:13.586 --> 00:15:16.090
They get routed to different
categories though there's

00:15:16.091 --> 00:15:18.760
a common entity called garbage.

00:15:18.761 --> 00:15:20.380
How do you associate the text in

00:15:20.381 --> 00:15:22.940
blue or yellow leading to
different classifiers?

00:15:22.941 --> 00:15:24.220
As I can say,

00:15:24.221 --> 00:15:26.080
because asking money for garbage.

00:15:26.081 --> 00:15:28.480
So, here are the larger context,

00:15:28.481 --> 00:15:33.365
in which this, the entity
garbage is being talked about.

00:15:33.366 --> 00:15:36.415
So, at least in InstiApp,

00:15:36.416 --> 00:15:38.035
what we are providing
right now is

00:15:38.036 --> 00:15:39.780
highlighting the section that is

00:15:39.781 --> 00:15:41.570
responsible for
a particular label showing

00:15:41.571 --> 00:15:43.460
up so that the user has clarity.

00:15:43.461 --> 00:15:45.035
Again, our goal is to arrive

00:15:45.036 --> 00:15:48.060
at a consensus as
soon as possible.

00:15:49.200 --> 00:15:52.070
Then, the other thing we
realize especially if

00:15:52.071 --> 00:15:54.670
it's SpeakUp, so SpeakUp,

00:15:54.671 --> 00:15:56.010
they're also happy writing

00:15:56.011 --> 00:16:00.455
their own small rules for
classification at their end.

00:16:00.456 --> 00:16:03.285
Because again the time required

00:16:03.286 --> 00:16:05.230
for a model to actually

00:16:05.231 --> 00:16:07.825
learn from your training data
could be large.

00:16:07.826 --> 00:16:10.010
So, can we use
labeling functions,

00:16:10.011 --> 00:16:12.460
a soft keyboard-based labelers?

00:16:12.461 --> 00:16:14.890
It's often very easy to associate

00:16:14.891 --> 00:16:16.890
the contextual keywords
with the entity,

00:16:16.891 --> 00:16:19.925
the actual drive,
the classification.

00:16:19.926 --> 00:16:21.900
How would we realize that

00:16:21.901 --> 00:16:24.225
the rules in themselves
might be very limited?

00:16:24.226 --> 00:16:27.010
When I say, "Garbage
not collected," I also

00:16:27.011 --> 00:16:30.855
probably mean that garbage
collection is being ignored,

00:16:30.856 --> 00:16:34.145
or there have been gaps
in garbage collection.

00:16:34.146 --> 00:16:35.970
So, how do you
expand the scope of

00:16:35.971 --> 00:16:38.365
a rule to a continuous
space, right?

00:16:38.366 --> 00:16:40.430
So, we'll also, I mean,

00:16:40.431 --> 00:16:42.595
certainly we kept research

00:16:42.596 --> 00:16:44.780
away from this far distance,
is that right?

00:16:44.781 --> 00:16:48.060
But we do realize
research is important,

00:16:48.061 --> 00:16:49.585
and in fact, we're quite excited

00:16:49.586 --> 00:16:50.900
about some of the work

00:16:50.901 --> 00:16:52.535
that has actually come
off from this work.

00:16:52.536 --> 00:16:55.620
How do you create
continuous labeling functions?

00:16:55.621 --> 00:16:58.080
The human gives
discrete labeling functions,

00:16:58.081 --> 00:16:59.070
the machine which is good at

00:16:59.071 --> 00:17:02.290
recall makes a labeling function,

00:17:02.291 --> 00:17:04.370
or treats it as a continuous one.

00:17:04.371 --> 00:17:08.090
This can have implications
in improving recall,

00:17:08.091 --> 00:17:10.840
the machines
continuously labeling

00:17:10.841 --> 00:17:13.580
space whereas a user is
very good at precision.

00:17:13.581 --> 00:17:14.795
Yeah, then you say, what are

00:17:14.796 --> 00:17:17.450
the ever-evolving problem list?

00:17:17.451 --> 00:17:20.370
Learning from
multi-line license plates

00:17:20.371 --> 00:17:21.520
for multiple vehicles in area.

00:17:21.521 --> 00:17:23.580
This came from ICMC,
IChangedMyCity.

00:17:23.581 --> 00:17:26.335
Sentiment analysis, this
is what SpeakUp wanted.

00:17:26.336 --> 00:17:28.560
Identifying "mood" or "tone"
of the complaint using NLP.

00:17:28.561 --> 00:17:30.140
Some of these complaints
where suicidal,

00:17:30.141 --> 00:17:31.490
I mean suicide notes.

00:17:31.491 --> 00:17:34.730
Almost. How do you

00:17:34.731 --> 00:17:37.865
prioritize the complaints
based on urgency?

00:17:37.866 --> 00:17:39.920
Some of them are
also interested in

00:17:39.921 --> 00:17:42.095
correcting the sentence grammar.

00:17:42.096 --> 00:17:45.510
Okay. Going forward,
what we would like,

00:17:45.511 --> 00:17:46.800
really like to do is more

00:17:46.801 --> 00:17:48.850
seriously work with
the CGNet Swara.

00:17:48.851 --> 00:17:53.175
We've been in talks with
CGNet Swara for some time,

00:17:53.176 --> 00:17:55.240
but I think now we've
stabilized that platform.

00:17:55.241 --> 00:17:56.910
We worked reasonably well for

00:17:56.911 --> 00:17:59.695
Indian languages,
and also mix code.

00:17:59.696 --> 00:18:04.250
We would like to see what we
can do with MSR Sys Sieve,

00:18:04.251 --> 00:18:08.290
and yes, that's for Venter.

00:18:08.291 --> 00:18:12.665
So, this is about
skilling in rural India,

00:18:12.666 --> 00:18:15.890
and this began with our
interaction with the MSR

00:18:15.891 --> 00:18:18.790
a year back to the Project Karya,

00:18:18.791 --> 00:18:22.255
which was about creating
digital jobs for rural India.

00:18:22.256 --> 00:18:25.295
So, the other part is,

00:18:25.296 --> 00:18:27.485
we have a very
strong collaboration

00:18:27.486 --> 00:18:29.840
between Department of
Computer Science at IIT Bombay,

00:18:29.841 --> 00:18:31.235
and there's a center for

00:18:31.236 --> 00:18:34.175
technology alternatives
